      <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-a:text-ceylon-green-700 prose-strong:text-gray-900">

        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 not-prose mb-8">Most travellers on Sri Lanka's south coast face the same question: Unawatuna or Mirissa? Both towns are on the south coast, both have curved sandy bays, both have restaurants, bars and the full apparatus of a beach holiday. But they're genuinely different places with different strengths, and the "better" one depends entirely on what you want from your time there. This guide compares them across every dimension that matters.</p>

        <div class="not-prose grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
          <div class="bg-ceylon-green-50 border border-ceylon-green-200 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="font-serif font-bold text-ceylon-green-900 text-lg mb-3">Unawatuna</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-ceylon-green-800">
              <li>✓ 15 min from <a href="/places/galle" class="underline">Galle Fort</a></li>
              <li>✓ More developed infrastructure</li>
              <li>✓ Best snorkelling at Jungle Beach</li>
              <li>✓ Better for budget travellers</li>
              <li>✓ More accommodation choice</li>
              <li>✗ Beach has some erosion issues</li>
              <li>✗ More crowded and commercial</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="font-serif font-bold text-blue-900 text-lg mb-3">Mirissa</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-blue-800">
              <li>✓ Sri Lanka's top whale watching base</li>
              <li>✓ More beautiful crescent beach</li>
              <li>✓ Younger, livelier nightlife scene</li>
              <li>✓ Better surf (Coconut Hill end)</li>
              <li>✓ More atmospheric at sunrise/sunset</li>
              <li>✗ No major heritage site nearby</li>
              <li>✗ More expensive than Unawatuna</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>The Beaches</h2>
        <p><strong>Mirissa wins on beach quality.</strong> Mirissa's main bay is a near-perfect crescent of pale gold sand, about 1.2km long, curling between the headland on the east and the rocky Coconut Hill on the west. The water is clear (though sea swells can make it choppy in the afternoon), the beach is relatively clean, and the photogenic curve of the bay with coconut palms and fishing boats gives it a classic tropical postcard quality. Sunrise at Mirissa, when the fishing fleet returns and the beach is quiet, is genuinely beautiful.</p>
        <p>Unawatuna's main beach has faced coastal erosion in recent years — portions of the beach are narrower than they once were, and some sections are affected by run-off from the town's drainage. The beach is functional and pleasant, but it doesn't have the natural drama of Mirissa's crescent. What Unawatuna does have is Jungle Beach — a 10-minute walk or short tuk-tuk ride over the headland from the main beach — a small, secluded cove with better snorkelling and far fewer people. For swimming and snorkelling, Jungle Beach is Unawatuna's trump card.</p>

        <h2>Whale Watching</h2>
        <p><strong>Mirissa wins decisively.</strong> Mirissa is the whale watching capital of Sri Lanka and one of the best places in the world to see blue whales from shore — the combination of a deep ocean trench close to the coast and year-round warm water means that blue whales, sperm whales, fin whales and spinner dolphins are regularly sighted from boats departing Mirissa harbour. The season runs roughly November to April, with peak sightings in January and February. Tours depart at 6am and return by midday (USD 35–55 per person). The blue whale sightings here — the largest animal that has ever lived, surfacing 30 metres from the boat — are among the most extraordinary wildlife encounters available anywhere on the Indian Ocean. If you're visiting between November and April, Mirissa is the right base for this alone.</p>
        <p>Whale watching tours also run from Dondra and Weligama, near Mirissa. Unawatuna offers no equivalent marine wildlife experience.</p>

