        <h2>Snorkelling</h2>
        <p><strong>Unawatuna (Jungle Beach) wins.</strong> Jungle Beach, the hidden cove on the far side of the headland from Unawatuna's main beach, has the best accessible snorkelling on this section of the south coast. The coral garden is reasonably healthy (it was damaged in the 2004 tsunami but has partially recovered), with reef fish, sea turtles and occasional juvenile sharks visible in clear conditions. Equipment can be rented on the beach. Unawatuna's main beach has some coral along its eastern end, but Jungle Beach is the real snorkelling spot.</p>
        <p>Mirissa's main beach has limited snorkelling — the water near the beach is often turbid from boat traffic and wave action. The Coconut Hill end offers some rock and coral, but snorkelling conditions at Mirissa are generally inferior to Jungle Beach. For dedicated snorkelling on the south coast, <a href="/places/hikkaduwa">Hikkaduwa</a> (40km west) remains the best option, with protected national park reef and regular turtle sightings.</p>

        <h2>Nightlife & Restaurants</h2>
        <p><strong>Mirissa wins for atmosphere; Unawatuna wins for choice.</strong> Mirissa has developed a genuinely lively beach bar scene centred on the main strip behind the beach — cocktail bars, fire dancers, beach parties and a cluster of popular restaurants that run until late. Paradise Beach Club and the cluster of bars near the harbour end of the beach are the social hub. The scene is younger and more animated than Unawatuna's.</p>
        <p>Unawatuna has more restaurants spread across a wider area, and being 15 minutes from Galle Fort means easy access to Galle's excellent restaurant scene in the evenings. The fort's dining options include some of the best food on the south coast. Unawatuna's own beach-strip restaurants are solid and plentiful; the nightlife is quieter than Mirissa. Both towns have options for every budget from cheap rice and curry to USD 20–30 restaurant meals.</p>

        <h2>Location & Day Trips</h2>
        <p><strong>Unawatuna wins clearly.</strong> Unawatuna's biggest practical advantage is its proximity to <a href="/places/galle">Galle Fort</a> — a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of Sri Lanka's most beautiful colonial-era landmarks. The Dutch fort, with its ramparts, cobbled streets, colonial architecture and excellent cafés and restaurants, can be explored as a half-day from Unawatuna. Mirissa is 35km further east and requires more planning for a Galle day trip. Unawatuna also gives easier access to Koggala (Japanese war cemetery, Handunugoda Tea Estate), Dalawella (stilt fishermen), and the south coast towards Weligama and Tangalle. For travellers who want to base themselves at one beach and make day trips around the south coast, Unawatuna's positioning between Galle (15km west) and Weligama (20km east) is ideal.</p>

        <h2>Accommodation & Prices</h2>
        <p>Both towns offer accommodation across a wide price range, from guesthouses for USD 15–25 per night to boutique hotels at USD 80–150. Mirissa has trended slightly more expensive in recent years as its whale watching reputation has attracted a more well-heeled visitor base. Both towns are considerably cheaper than Maldives-style luxury or Colombo city hotels for comparable quality. Booking in advance is essential December–March (peak season) — walking up to either town without a reservation in January is unreliable. The back streets behind the beach at Unawatuna hide a good stock of budget guesthouses that are cheaper than anything on the beachfront strip.</p>

        <h2>The Verdict: Which Should You Choose?</h2>
        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 my-8 border border-gray-200">
          <h3 class="font-serif font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-4">Choose Unawatuna if:</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700 mb-6">
            <li>→ You want to be close to Galle Fort and explore the area</li>
            <li>→ Snorkelling is a priority (Jungle Beach)</li>
            <li>→ Budget matters — slightly more affordable options</li>
            <li>→ You want more restaurant and café variety</li>
            <li>→ You're travelling as a couple wanting culture + beach</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="font-serif font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-4">Choose Mirissa if:</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <li>→ Whale watching is a must (November–April window)</li>
            <li>→ You want the most photogenic beach on the south coast</li>
            <li>→ Nightlife and the beach bar scene appeals</li>
            <li>→ Surfing is a priority (Coconut Hill end)</li>
            <li>→ You're visiting January–February for peak marine wildlife</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>The honest answer is: visit both. They're only 35km apart. A south coast itinerary based from Unawatuna (3 nights) and moving east to Mirissa (2 nights) gives you Galle Fort, Jungle Beach snorkelling, whale watching and Mirissa's sunset beach in one logical route that leads naturally towards Tangalle and Yala.</p>

        <h2>Getting Between Them</h2>
        <p>Unawatuna and Mirissa are connected by the coastal A2 highway. A tuk-tuk between the two takes 45 minutes and costs LKR 1,500–2,000. The train from Unawatuna (Galle station, 3km away) to Weligama (7km east of Mirissa) runs regularly and costs LKR 40–60; the coastal train ride between Galle and Matara is one of Sri Lanka's most scenic routes with Indian Ocean views from the window. Buses on the A2 are the cheapest option (LKR 80–100) but slow. Most travellers either take the train or hire a tuk-tuk for the journey.</p>

